//! lotus/stereo_smooth_trim.rye -- ALES154: the smoothed silence trim carried into stereo -- smooth EACH channel's runs through ALES153 BEFORE intersecting the two
//! silences, so the joint strip declicks the pause and keeps the breath while the stereo image stays whole. ALES102 named the mono `smooth_trim`: segment a clip, smooth
//! its runs with `min_run`, then strip the SMOOTHED silence -- one knob that turns a literal silence remover into a speech-aware one (a short voiced tick buried in a pause
//! is relabeled silent and stripped; a short silent gap inside speech is relabeled voiced and kept). ALES148 carried the RAW silence trim into stereo through the SILENCE
//! INTERSECTION: cut only a frame silent in BOTH channels, so the two speakers leave equal length and the image never tears. This rung fuses the two -- it smooths the raw
//! segmentation of EACH channel first (ALES153, read-only over the labels), then intersects the SMOOTHED silences and cuts the joint dead air in lockstep.
//!
//! The rule, stated once -- smooth then intersect: segment BOTH channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, smooth EACH channel's runs through ALES153 with the one
//! shared `min_run`, mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is `.silent` in BOTH SMOOTHED channels, and cut exactly those frames from BOTH channels in lockstep, walking
//! back to front. Smoothing is per-channel and read-only (ALES153 proved the two channels smooth apart, touching no PCM), and ALES101's coverage law guarantees each
//! smoothed run list tiles its channel's span exactly -- so every jointly-silent frame is a valid span in both original clips, cut exactly as ALES148 cuts. A frame silent
//! in only one smoothed channel is KEPT; a short tick that smooths to silent on both is stripped; a short gap that smooths to voiced on either is kept.
//!
//! The crux -- this rung is ALES148 reading ALES153's SMOOTHED labels instead of the raw ones. It invents no new measurement and no new sample: it reads its silences
//! through ALES147, rewrites them through ALES153 (which composes ALES101 twice), and removes whole frames through ALES2's `cut`, fabricating nothing and reordering
//! nothing. What is new is only the fusion -- smoothing each channel before the intersection -- and its whole honesty is the two seams it inherits: `min_run = 0` is exactly
//! ALES148 (identity smoothing), and running the proven mono `smooth_trim` (ALES102) per channel would DESYNC the pair, where this cuts once for both and keeps them
//! balanced. Not monotone in `min_run` (a larger `min_run` removes more clicks yet keeps more gaps), the honest twin of ALES102's own non-monotonicity, now across two
//! independent silences fused into one joint cut.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES154.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-060518_fill-ales154-lotus-stereo-smooth-trim.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148) -- the RAW stereo silence trim (the SILENCE INTERSECTION and lockstep cut), the min-zero twin, imported by the selftest
//! to prove the min-zero law; lotus/stereo_smooth_runs.rye (ALES153) -- the stereo run-length smoother, its `smooth_runs_stereo` run once to smooth BOTH channels' runs and
//! its `StereoSmoothError` reused whole; lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147) -- the stereo segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences over one shared frame grid, its
//! `segment_stereo`, `StereoSegments`, and `StereoSegmentError` reused whole; lotus/segment.rye (ALES95) -- the mono segmenter and `max_segments`; lotus/smooth_trim.rye
//! (ALES102) -- the mono smoothed trim, imported by the selftest to prove the LOCKSTEP law (that running it per channel would desync the pair); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the
//! StereoClip and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, its `cut`, `max_clip`, and EditError reused whole; lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94) -- the `.silent`
//! verdict this smooths and intersects.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. This rung WRITES -- it edits both clips -- yet
//! only by removing whole jointly-(smoothed-)silent frames through ALES2's own cut, on both channels in lockstep; it fabricates no samples and reorders nothing, and the
//! smoothing it adds is read-only over the labels. The smoothing is ALES101's CAUSAL hold per channel -- it cannot rescue a too-short OPENING run -- and this rung is no
//! adaptive VAD. NOT a transcript, not a diarization; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_smooth_trim.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const segment = @import("segment.rye");
const stereo_segment = @import("stereo_segment.rye");
const stereo_smooth_runs = @import("stereo_smooth_runs.rye");
const stereo_trim_silence = @import("stereo_trim_silence.rye");
const smooth_trim = @import("smooth_trim.rye");
const smooth_runs = @import("smooth_runs.rye");

pub const stereo_smooth_trim_version = "20260815.060518";

/// The most frames a span can carry -- one per sample, when `frame_len` is 1 -- bounded by the clip's own sample ceiling. Names the jointly-silent mask's size once.
const max_frames: u32 = timeline.max_clip;

/// The stereo smoothed trim's faults are the stereo segmenter's OWN faults (ALES147's StereoSegmentError, itself ALES95's SegmentError: band/floor faults, BadFrame, the
/// honest unreachable SegmentsFull), the smoother's OWN fault (ALES153's StereoSmoothError, only that same unreachable SegmentsFull), and the editor's OWN faults (ALES2's
/// EditError from cut) -- each reused by name. The fusion invents none of its own: `min_run` is a plain count with no illegal value (a huge min_run coalesces a channel to
/// one run), so a keeper reads one error vocabulary across the read, the smooth, and the edit.
pub const StereoSmoothTrimError = stereo_segment.StereoSegmentError || stereo_smooth_runs.StereoSmoothError || timeline.EditError;

/// Remove every JOINTLY-silent frame from both channels of `sc` AFTER smoothing each channel's segmentation, so a short voiced tick that smooths to silent on both is
/// stripped with the pause, a short silent gap that smooths to voiced on either is kept, and the stereo image stays balanced. Segments BOTH channels of the whole clip
/// `[0, len)` into frames of `frame_len` through ALES147, smooths EACH channel's runs through ALES153 with `min_run`, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is
/// `.silent` in BOTH SMOOTHED channels, and cuts exactly those frames from BOTH channels through ALES2's `cut`, walking back to front so every earlier frame's index stays
/// valid. Validates the whole span, band, floor, and frame length through ALES147's own up-front checks BEFORE any cut; on refusal both channels are untouched. `min_run = 0`
/// is exactly ALES148. An empty clip refuses `BadFrame`.
pub fn stereo_smooth_trim(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    frame_len: u32,
    t_low: i32,
    t_high: i32,
    silence_floor: u32,
    voice_split: u32,
    min_run: u32,
) StereoSmoothTrimError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the whole edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    const original_len: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // Segment BOTH channels once over one shared frame grid. ALES147 runs all of its (and ALES95's) validation here -- band/span precheck, the floor ceiling, the dividing
    // frame length, the empty-clip BadFrame -- read-only, before a single sample is cut. On refusal it leaves the source untouched, so both channels are too.
    var segs = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
    try stereo_segment.segment_stereo(sc, &segs, 0, original_len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, silence_floor, voice_split);

    // invariant: segmenting a non-empty clip yields at least one run per channel that tiles it -- ALES147's own postcondition, relied on here.
    assert(segs.left.len >= 1 and segs.left.len <= segment.max_segments);
    assert(segs.right.len >= 1 and segs.right.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // Smooth EACH channel's runs before intersecting -- the ONLY difference from ALES148. ALES153 composes ALES101 twice, once per side, read-only over the labels (no PCM
    // touched), keeping the two channels apart. ALES101's coverage law guarantees each smoothed run list tiles its channel's span exactly, so every smoothed silent span is
    // a valid original index.
    var smoothed = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
    try stereo_smooth_runs.smooth_runs_stereo(&segs, &smoothed, min_run);

    // invariant: smoothing a non-empty segmentation yields at least one run per channel that still tiles the clip -- ALES101's coverage law, per side.
    assert(smoothed.left.len >= 1 and smoothed.left.len <= segment.max_segments);
    assert(smoothed.right.len >= 1 and smoothed.right.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // The frames tile [0, original_len) exactly -- the segmenter refused a non-dividing or zero frame length, so this division is exact and at least one.
    const frames: u32 = original_len / frame_len;
    // invariant: the frame count is real and never exceeds the mask that records it.
    assert(frames >= 1 and frames <= max_frames);

    // Forward pass -- the SILENCE INTERSECTION over the SMOOTHED labels. Walk the frames left to right, advancing one cursor per channel across its own smoothed tiling run
    // list, and mark a frame jointly-silent ONLY where it is `.silent` in both SMOOTHED channels. A frame silent in one smoothed channel alone is left unmarked -- the other
    // speaker's content keeps it. Count the jointly-silent samples so the length postcondition can be asserted exactly.
    var joint_silent = [_]bool{false} ** max_frames;
    var joint_total: u32 = 0;
    var li: u32 = 0;
    var ri: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        // The smoothed runs tile [0, original_len) in order and frame starts only increase, so each cursor advances monotonically to the run covering this frame's start.
        while (li + 1 < smoothed.left.len and smoothed.left.items[li].start + smoothed.left.items[li].count <= fs) : (li += 1) {}
        while (ri + 1 < smoothed.right.len and smoothed.right.items[ri].start + smoothed.right.items[ri].count <= fs) : (ri += 1) {}
        // invariant: each cursor names the smoothed run that actually covers fs -- the runs tile the span, so exactly one always does.
        assert(smoothed.left.items[li].start <= fs and fs < smoothed.left.items[li].start + smoothed.left.items[li].count);
        assert(smoothed.right.items[ri].start <= fs and fs < smoothed.right.items[ri].start + smoothed.right.items[ri].count);
        if (smoothed.left.items[li].verdict == .silent and smoothed.right.items[ri].verdict == .silent) {
            joint_silent[k] = true;
            joint_total += frame_len;
        }
    }
    // invariant: the joint silence removed can never exceed the clip it came from.
    assert(joint_total <= original_len);

    // Backward pass -- cut every jointly-silent frame from BOTH channels in lockstep. Back-to-front is the whole trick: a cut shifts only samples after it, so an earlier
    // frame keeps its original start index. Cutting the SAME span from both channels keeps them balanced at every step; the bounds are asserted, so neither cut can fault
    // and leave the pair half-trimmed.
    var b: u32 = frames;
    while (b > 0) {
        b -= 1;
        if (joint_silent[b]) {
            const fs: u32 = b * frame_len;
            // invariant: the frame lies wholly inside both current clips -- it did before any cut, and only later frames (already cut) sat after it.
            assert(fs + frame_len <= sc.left.len and fs + frame_len <= sc.right.len);
            try timeline.cut(&sc.left, fs, frame_len);
            try timeline.cut(&sc.right, fs, frame_len);
        }
    }

    // invariant: both channels lost exactly the jointly-smoothed-silent samples and leave balanced -- the length holds identical on both, the original minus the joint silence.
    assert(sc.left.len == original_len - joint_total);
    assert(sc.right.len == original_len - joint_total);
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SMOOTH-TRIM LAW -- stereo_smooth_trim equals a by-hand keep of the frames NOT jointly-SMOOTHED-silent, per channel, across many frame lengths,
//     floors, splits, and min_run values (the crux); THE MIN-ZERO LAW -- stereo_smooth_trim(min=0) equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both channels;
//     THE DECLICK LAW -- a short voiced tick jointly buried in silence, at a min_run past the tick, is removed on both, strictly shorter than the min-zero trim; THE
//     KEEP-GAP LAW -- a short silent gap jointly between speech, at a min_run past the gap, is kept on both, strictly longer than the min-zero trim; THE BALANCE / LOCKSTEP
//     LAW -- both channels leave equal length, where running mono smooth_trim per channel would DESYNC them; THE DEGENERATE LAW -- all-joint-silent empties both, no-joint-
//     silent unchanged on both; THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a shared BadFrame / BadThreshold / BadFloor refuses before any cut, both channels untouched and balanced -----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// The verdict of the smoothed run that covers sample `fs`, read from a mono segmentation by finding the run that covers it -- an independent reference to the cursor walk
/// inside `stereo_smooth_trim`, so the crux is proven against a second implementation.
fn frame_verdict(segs: *const segment.Segments, fs: u32) segment.Segment {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < segs.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (segs.items[i].start <= fs and fs < segs.items[i].start + segs.items[i].count) return segs.items[i];
    }
    unreachable; // the smoothed runs tile the span, so a frame start always lands in one
}

/// The hand-built expectation the stereo smoothed trimmer is checked against -- segment BOTH channels of the ORIGINAL, smooth EACH channel's runs with min_run, then, for
/// each frame NOT silent in BOTH smoothed channels, keep that frame's samples on each channel. The trimmed pair must equal this exactly, both channels the same length.
fn by_hand(out: *pan.StereoClip, src: *const pan.StereoClip, frame_len: u32, t_low: i32, t_high: i32, floor: u32, split: u32, min_run: u32) !void {
    var ls = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.left, &ls, 0, src.left.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    var rs = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.right, &rs, 0, src.right.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    var lsm = segment.Segments{};
    try smooth_runs.smooth_runs(&ls, &lsm, min_run);
    var rsm = segment.Segments{};
    try smooth_runs.smooth_runs(&rs, &rsm, min_run);
    const left_samples = src.left.samples();
    const right_samples = src.right.samples();
    const frames: u32 = src.left.len / frame_len;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        const l_silent = frame_verdict(&lsm, fs).verdict == .silent;
        const r_silent = frame_verdict(&rsm, fs).verdict == .silent;
        if (!(l_silent and r_silent)) {
            try timeline.splice(&out.left, out.left.len, left_samples[fs .. fs + frame_len]);
            try timeline.splice(&out.right, out.right.len, right_samples[fs .. fs + frame_len]);
        }
    }
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const band_low: i32 = -1000;
    const band_high: i32 = 1000;

    // Four frames of four samples, the two channels carrying DIFFERENT silences: frame 1 is quiet on the LEFT yet loud on the RIGHT, frame 2 is quiet on BOTH. The same
    // shape ALES148 used, so the two rungs' silence intersection is compared on common ground before the smoothing is exercised.
    const left_sig = [_]i16{
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 0 — loud on both (voiced)
        40,    -40,    40,     -40, // frame 1 — LEFT quiet (silent), right loud
        30,    -30,    30,     -30, // frame 2 — quiet on BOTH (jointly silent)
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 3 — loud on both
    };
    const right_sig = [_]i16{
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 0 — loud
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 1 — RIGHT loud (keeps the frame)
        50,    -50,    50,     -50, // frame 2 — quiet (silent)
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 3 — loud
    };

    // 1. THE STEREO SMOOTH-TRIM LAW -- over many frame lengths, floors, splits, AND min_run values, stereo_smooth_trim equals the hand-built keep of the frames NOT jointly-
    //    SMOOTHED-silent, per channel, and the two channels leave exactly equal length.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 }; // each divides 16
        const floors = [_]u32{ 100, 500, 1000, 5000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4 };
        const mins = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 100 };
        var li: u32 = 0;
        while (li < flens.len) : (li += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var ci: u32 = 0;
                    while (ci < mins.len) : (ci += 1) {
                        var got = pan.StereoClip{};
                        try load_stereo(&got, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                        var src = pan.StereoClip{};
                        try load_stereo(&src, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                        var want = pan.StereoClip{};
                        try stereo_smooth_trim(&got, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si], mins[ci]);
                        try by_hand(&want, &src, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si], mins[ci]);
                        if (!eq(got.left.samples(), want.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotHandTrimmed;
                        if (!eq(got.right.samples(), want.right.samples())) return error.RightNotHandTrimmed;
                        if (got.left.len != got.right.len) return error.TrimmedUnbalanced;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 2. THE MIN-ZERO LAW -- over many frame lengths, floors, and splits, stereo_smooth_trim(min=0) equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both channels;
    //    min_run zero is the identity smoothing, so the fusion collapses to the raw stereo trim and this rung is a strict generalization.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 };
        const floors = [_]u32{ 100, 500, 1000, 5000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4 };
        var li: u32 = 0;
        while (li < flens.len) : (li += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var smoothed = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&smoothed, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    var raw = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&raw, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    try stereo_smooth_trim(&smoothed, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si], 0);
                    try stereo_trim_silence.stereo_trim_silence(&raw, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    if (!eq(smoothed.left.samples(), raw.left.samples())) return error.MinZeroLeftNotTrim;
                    if (!eq(smoothed.right.samples(), raw.right.samples())) return error.MinZeroRightNotTrim;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 3. THE DECLICK LAW -- a short voiced tick buried in JOINT silence, identical on both channels, at a min_run past the tick, is removed on both. silent(8)-voiced tick(4)-
    //    silent(8) on both: the min-zero trim keeps the 4 tick samples (jointly voiced), smooth with min_run 8 relabels the tick silent on both, so it becomes joint dead
    //    air and the whole clip empties -- strictly shorter than the min-zero trim.
    {
        const ticked = [_]i16{
            40,    -40,   40,    -40, 40, -40, 40, -40, // silent (8)
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced tick (4) — a click
            30,    -30,   30,    -30, 30, -30, 30, -30, // silent (8)
        };
        var raw = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&raw, &ticked, &ticked);
        try stereo_smooth_trim(&raw, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 0); // min-zero: the raw stereo trim keeps the joint tick
        if (raw.left.len != 4 or raw.right.len != 4) return error.RawTrimShouldKeepTick;

        var smoothed = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&smoothed, &ticked, &ticked);
        try stereo_smooth_trim(&smoothed, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 8); // 8 > the 4-sample tick
        if (smoothed.left.len != 0 or smoothed.right.len != 0) return error.DeclickShouldEmpty; // the click is gone on both
        if (smoothed.left.len >= raw.left.len) return error.DeclickNotShorter; // strictly shorter than the min-zero trim
    }

    // 4. THE KEEP-GAP LAW -- a short silent gap between two words, identical on both channels, at a min_run past the gap, is kept on both. voiced(8)-silent gap(4)-voiced(8)
    //    on both: the min-zero trim removes the joint gap (16 samples run together), smooth with min_run 8 relabels the gap voiced on both, so nothing is jointly silent and
    //    all 20 samples are kept -- strictly longer than the min-zero trim.
    {
        const gapped = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced (8)
            30,    -30,   30,    -30, // silent gap (4) — a breath
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced (8)
        };
        var raw = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&raw, &gapped, &gapped);
        try stereo_smooth_trim(&raw, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 0); // min-zero: the raw stereo trim closes the joint gap
        if (raw.left.len != 16 or raw.right.len != 16) return error.RawTrimShouldCutGap;

        var smoothed = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&smoothed, &gapped, &gapped);
        try stereo_smooth_trim(&smoothed, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 8); // 8 > the 4-sample gap
        if (!eq(smoothed.left.samples(), &gapped) or !eq(smoothed.right.samples(), &gapped)) return error.KeepGapChanged; // every sample kept on both
        if (smoothed.left.len <= raw.left.len) return error.KeepGapNotLonger; // strictly longer than the min-zero trim
    }

    // 5. THE LOCKSTEP LAW -- the reason the stereo fusion exists. Running the proven mono smooth_trim (ALES102) on each channel APART would DESYNC the pair here: the left,
    //    quiet in frames 1 and 2, loses both to length 8; the right, quiet only in frame 2, loses one to length 12. The stereo smoothed trim keeps both at 12 by cutting
    //    only the joint SMOOTHED silence, so the stereo image never tears. min_run small enough not to change the frame-scale verdicts.
    {
        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &left_sig);
        try smooth_trim.smooth_trim(&ml, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 1);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &right_sig);
        try smooth_trim.smooth_trim(&mr, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 1);
        if (ml.len == mr.len) return error.MonoDidNotDesync; // the honest premise: per-channel mono smooth_trim DOES desync here
        if (ml.len != 8 or mr.len != 12) return error.MonoDesyncUnexpected;

        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 1);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.StereoTornApart;
        if (sc.left.len != 12) return error.StereoWrongLength; // the joint silence is one frame, not two
    }

    // 6. THE DEGENERATE LAW, all-joint-silent -- a master quiet on BOTH channels everywhere trims to empty on both, whatever the min_run (a wholly-silent channel coalesces
    //    to one silent run the smoother leaves silent).
    {
        const quiet_l = [_]i16{ 40, -40, 40, -40, 30, -30, 30, -30 };
        const quiet_r = [_]i16{ 41, -41, 41, -41, 31, -31, 31, -31 };
        const mins = [_]u32{ 0, 4, 100 };
        var ci: u32 = 0;
        while (ci < mins.len) : (ci += 1) {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &quiet_l, &quiet_r);
            try stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, mins[ci]);
            if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.AllJointSilentNotEmpty;
        }
    }

    // 7. THE DEGENERATE LAW, no-joint-silent -- a master loud on at least one channel in every frame is unchanged on both, whatever the min_run. Here every frame is loud on
    //    the right, so NOTHING is jointly silent even though the left goes quiet in the middle, and the smoother cannot create a joint silence that was not there.
    {
        const some_l = [_]i16{ 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 40, -40, 40, -40 };
        const loud_r = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000 };
        const mins = [_]u32{ 0, 4, 100 };
        var ci: u32 = 0;
        while (ci < mins.len) : (ci += 1) {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &some_l, &loud_r);
            try stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 1, mins[ci]);
            if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &some_l)) return error.NoJointSilentLeftChanged;
            if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &loud_r)) return error.NoJointSilentRightChanged;
        }
    }

    // 8a. AN EMPTY CLIP refuses BadFrame (nothing to trim), both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        if (stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 4)) |_| return error.EmptySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongEmpty;
        if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyMutated;
    }

    // 8b. A RAGGED FRAME LENGTH -- one that does not divide the clip -- refuses BadFrame, both channels untouched.
    {
        const five_l = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        const five_r = [_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &five_l, &five_r);
        if (stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 4)) |_| return error.RaggedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongRagged;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &five_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &five_r)) return error.RaggedMutated;
    }

    // 8c. AN INVERTED BAND refuses BadThreshold (through ALES147's precheck), both channels untouched.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 2, 1000, -1000, 500, 2, 4)) |_| return error.InvertedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongInverted;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.InvertedMutated;
    }

    // 8d. AN ILLEGAL FLOOR -- above the peak ceiling -- refuses BadFloor, both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_smooth_trim(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 32769, 2, 4)) |_| return error.BadFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFloor) return error.WrongBadFloor;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.BadFloorMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadFloorUnbalanced;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-smooth-trim: the SMOOTHED SILENCE TRIM CARRIED INTO STEREO — smooth EACH channel's runs through ALES153 BEFORE intersecting the two " ++
        "silences, so the joint strip declicks the pause and keeps the breath while the stereo image stays whole. ALES102 smooths a mono clip's runs then strips the " ++
        "SMOOTHED silence; ALES148 carried the RAW stereo trim through the SILENCE INTERSECTION (cut only a frame silent in BOTH channels, so the two speakers leave " ++
        "equal length). This rung fuses them: segment both channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, smooth EACH channel's runs through ALES153 with one " ++
        "shared min_run (read-only over the labels, the two channels smoothing apart), mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in BOTH SMOOTHED channels, " ++
        "and cut exactly those frames from both channels in lockstep, walking back to front so every earlier frame's index stays valid. ALES101's coverage law " ++
        "guarantees each smoothed run list tiles its channel's span exactly, so every jointly-silent frame is a valid original span cut exactly as ALES148 cuts. The " ++
        "crux: this rung is ALES148 reading ALES153's SMOOTHED labels instead of the raw ones — it invents no new measurement and no new sample, reading its silences " ++
        "through ALES147, rewriting them through ALES153, and removing whole frames through ALES2's cut. What is new is only the fusion, and its whole honesty is two " ++
        "seams: min_run 0 is exactly ALES148 (identity smoothing), and running the proven mono smooth_trim per channel would DESYNC the pair (left losing two frames to " ++
        "length 8, right one to length 12) where this cuts once for both and keeps them at 12. Proven: the stereo smooth-trim law (the result equals a by-hand keep of " ++
        "the frames not jointly-SMOOTHED-silent, per channel, across many frame lengths, floors, splits, and min_run values, both channels equal length); the min-zero " ++
        "law (min=0 equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both); the declick law (a short voiced tick jointly buried in silence, at a min_run past the " ++
        "tick, removed on both, strictly shorter than the min-zero trim); the keep-gap law (a short silent gap jointly between speech, at a min_run past the gap, kept " ++
        "on both, strictly longer); the lockstep law (per-channel mono smooth_trim desyncs to 8 and 12 while the stereo smoothed trim holds both at 12); the degenerate " ++
        "laws (all-joint-silent empties both, a master loud on at least one channel per frame unchanged on both, whatever the min_run); and the atomicity laws (an " ++
        "empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name — BadFrame, BadFrame, BadThreshold, BadFloor — both channels " ++
        "untouched and still balanced). Not monotone in min_run, the honest twin of ALES102's own non-monotonicity, now across two independent silences fused into one " ++
        "joint cut. Purely local — it edits only by removing whole jointly-smoothed-silent frames through cut on both channels in lockstep, the smoothing read-only " ++
        "over the labels, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing; a causal hold, NOT an adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no " ++
        "real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
